\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:discussion}\label{sec:selection_method}\label{sec:conclusion}

\paragraph{Why test-time scaling fails for structured outputs.}
For discrete-answer reasoning, sample diversity reflects diverse reasoning paths; for structured outputs, output convergence (degeneracy $12$--$68$\%) eliminates candidate diversity while signal alignment collapse ($\alpha{\to}0$) prevents ranking---a \textit{capability-diversity tension} that persists across model families (5.5--74\%; Table~\ref{tab:frontier_sc_cross}) and scales (4B--72B; \S\ref{sec:scaling}).
On CoNLL, pretrained MV captures ${\sim}98$\% of oracle headroom versus ${<}2$\% finetuned (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale}), directly demonstrating capability-induced convergence.
A free-form decoding ablation confirms this is not a constrained decoding artifact: CoNLL free-form degeneracy is $59.3$\% versus $56.7$\% constrained ($\Delta{=}2.6$\,pp); SciERC score compression persists (output-std ratio $0.97{\times}$; Appendix~\ref{app:freeform_ablation}), indicating degeneracy is model-intrinsic rather than grammar-induced.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
The cascade provides a pre-deployment diagnostic: measure degeneracy and LP-F1 alignment with $N{=}8$ samples before investing in $N{\times}$ inference.
When degeneracy exceeds $\theta_{\text{crit}}{=}0.70$, aggregation is unlikely to help; when both conditions are favorable ($D{<}22\%$, LP $\rho{>}0.2$), exploratory entity-level construction offers modest gains.
These findings are primarily validated on 7--14B fine-tuned NER models; generalization to other structured tasks requires further investigation.

\paragraph{Conclusion.}
We characterize when test-time compute scaling fails for structured prediction, using NER and RE as case studies.
The correlation-selection gap is governed by output convergence and signal alignment collapse, not signal quality per se; our contribution is empirical characterization.
The exploratory bypasses partially circumvent the bottleneck at entity granularity, but the capability-diversity tension remains an open challenge for structured outputs.
